{"claim":"NFPA 72 (2022) requires that where speech intelligibility is required, voice evacuation systems achieve a Speech Transmission Index of at least 0.45, or a Common Intelligibility Scale score of at least 0.7, throughout the area of coverage.","source_title":"NFPA 72, National Fire Alarm and Signaling Code, 2022 edition, Section 18.4.11 and Annex D","source_citation":"National Fire Protection Association. NFPA 72, National Fire Alarm and Signaling Code, 2022 edition. Sections 18.4.11 and Annex D (speech intelligibility, STI/CIS thresholds).","source_type":"standard","is_primary":true,"page_or_section":"§18.4.11; Annex D","supporting_excerpt":"Where required, an average speech transmission index (STI) of not less than 0.45 or common intelligibility scale (CIS) of not less than 0.70 shall be achieved throughout the area of coverage; STIPA is the specified measurement method (per Annex D).","source_date":"2022","data_vintage":"2022","airport_or_entity":"national code (adopted by reference in Virginia and D.C. construction/fire codes)","units":"STI (unitless 0–1)","denominator":"per area of coverage","caveat":"NFPA 72 leaves the specific verification method and threshold selection to the Authority Having Jurisdiction; adoption in Virginia and D.C. codes carries local amendments that should be confirmed against the current edition.","confidence":"high"}
{"claim":"28 CFR §35.160 requires a Title II public entity to take appropriate steps to ensure that communications with people with disabilities are as effective as communications with others, and to give primary consideration to the aids and services requested by the individual.","source_title":"28 CFR §35.160 — General (ADA Title II, Subpart E, Communications)","source_url":"https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-28/chapter-I/part-35/subpart-E/section-35.160","source_type":"federal_regulation","is_primary":true,"page_or_section":"28 CFR §35.160(a)–(b)","supporting_excerpt":"A public entity shall take appropriate steps to ensure that communications with applicants, participants, members of the public, and companions with disabilities are as effective as communications with others… In determining what types of auxiliary aids and services are necessary, a public entity shall give primary consideration to the requests of individuals with disabilities.","source_date":"current eCFR","data_vintage":"regulation in force","airport_or_entity":"applies to MWAA as a Title II public entity","units":null,"denominator":null,"caveat":"'Primary consideration' is a legal standard, not an absolute requirement to provide the requested aid; the entity may choose an alternative if it is equally effective.","confidence":"high"}
